WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.453
- Why are you talking to the police

00:00:01.453 --> 00:00:02.943
without a lawyer present?

00:00:04.428 --> 00:00:05.428
Ugh, that's,

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:08.167
"Law &amp; Order"'s gonna drive me insane!

00:00:09.407 --> 00:00:11.050
"Law &amp; Order" is back, baby!

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:12.360
I grew up with "Law &amp; Order".

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.640
I love "Law &amp; Order",

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.500
and I was very sad when it got canceled.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.670
It's been off the air for
over a decade, a decade!

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:21.880
And why would you cancel
regular "Law &amp; Order"?

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.290
It was a tent pole!

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:24.123
- Why?

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:27.150
It was a tent pole, a tent pole!

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.020
- I'm super happy that it's back,

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:30.560
though I don't know if it holds up.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.550
But let's get to it, let's dig in.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.060
- [Narrator] Two separate
yet equally important groups,

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.950
the police who investigate crime

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.800
and the district attorneys
who prosecute the offenders.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.333
These are their stories.

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:42.520
(cell clanging)

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.810
- Diane, I spent three years,

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.110
two months, and 11 days in prison

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:49.620
for a crime that I did not commit.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.610
Like many people of color,

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.670
I was wrongfully charged
and wrongfully convicted.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.290
- Okay, so right off the bat,

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.790
we have a guy who is a
stand-in for Bill Cosby,

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:01.950
and right off the bat,

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.250
this video is completely demonetized.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.076
Great, wonderful.

00:01:06.076 --> 00:01:09.409
(soft electronic music)

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.362
- Over here.

00:01:12.362 --> 00:01:13.862
Name's Henry King.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:15.720
- The singer?

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.040
- [Technician] Among other things.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:17.900
- All right, so clearly,

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.010
it's going to be one of this guy's victims

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.010
that got revenge and killed this guy.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.440
This is a classic "Law &amp; Order",

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.010
just ripping it right from the headlines

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.750
and just laying it out on a platter, here,

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.909
is my guess where this is gonna go.

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:36.159
(humming "Law &amp; Order" theme song)

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:41.820
- All right, "Law &amp; Order"'s back!

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:42.653
I love it!

00:01:42.653 --> 00:01:44.740
- Mrs. King, where were you tonight

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.520
between the hours of nine and 11?

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.883
- Okay, so this is the widow.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.200
She's clearly a suspect.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.450
Why doesn't she have a lawyer?

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:53.960
Have a lawyer if the police are talking,

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.980
and their first question
is, "Where were you?"

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.490
They're asking to find
inculpatory evidence.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.660
You can afford a lawyer, get a lawyer!

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:02.493
Duh!

00:02:02.493 --> 00:02:04.790
- Since your husband was
released from prison,

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.170
have there been any problems?

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.940
Have you noticed anything unusual?

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.120
- I think this is unusual,

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:12.193
that you're talking to the
police without a lawyer present.

00:02:12.193 --> 00:02:15.870
- Just found a text on King's
phone sent two days ago.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.167
It says, "It's over, I'm coming for you."

00:02:23.432 --> 00:02:24.419
(cell clanging)

00:02:24.419 --> 00:02:27.100
- All right, so in classic
"Law &amp; Order" fashion,

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.060
this is going to be a hot lead

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:30.370
that turns out to be a red herring.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:31.363
I guarantee it.

00:02:32.316 --> 00:02:34.380
- Hey, hey, how you doing?

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.260
We're looking for a guy
named Shabazz Walker.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.910
Seen him?

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:38.743
- Yeah, a'ight.

00:02:38.743 --> 00:02:39.730
- Yo, yo, I'm not done talking to you.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:41.660
- Yo, kiss my ass!

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:42.793
- What'd you say to me?

00:02:42.793 --> 00:02:44.113
(people yelling)

00:02:44.113 --> 00:02:45.110
- Ooh, okay.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.660
All right, now, come on.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.050
There are circumstances where
the police can detain you,

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.340
but this guy isn't a suspect.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:51.613
This isn't even a material witness.

00:02:51.613 --> 00:02:53.690
This is some random person

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.450
that this guy has found on the street,

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.700
and now the police are
laying hands on this guy.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.261
That's clearly a
constitutional violation, here.

00:03:02.261 --> 00:03:03.522
(people chattering)

00:03:03.522 --> 00:03:05.110
- Are you kidding me?

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.710
These young kids, they got no respect.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:09.260
- Oh, so he disrespected you
and you just grabbed him?

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.520
You don't get to do that,

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:14.990
policeman "Burn Notice" guy! (laughs)

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:16.030
- It's like a free pass.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.000
- Yeah, I'm not sure
what you mean by that.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.150
- Uh, I mean, I'm white, he's black.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.910
I say the wrong thing
and my career's over.

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:23.743
- Maybe.

00:03:23.743 --> 00:03:25.190
- Maybe, is there another
way of looking at this?

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:26.700
- Hey, Frank, you came at him hot.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.100
- Came at him hot?

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.390
He laid hands on him!

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:32.520
He restricted, that's assault!

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:33.680
That's more than coming in hot!

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.630
- Well, if we're gonna
keep this thing going,

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.330
you gotta know something about me.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.470
I speak my mind,

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.840
probably about things I
shouldn't speak my mind about.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.330
But it's just how I'm wired.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.520
- I mean, that's just
good writing, right there.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.137
Just replacing all the subtext with text.

00:03:50.137 --> 00:03:52.567
"I speak my mind,

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:54.950
"probably about things I
shouldn't speak my mind about."

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.333
I mean, that's just A+.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:03:59.283
- Look at this.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.690
- I know her.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:04.053
She's a prosecutor, her name's Jamie Ross.

00:04:04.978 --> 00:04:06.660
(cell clanging)

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:07.860
- Henry King has been harassing me

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:09.310
since he was released from prison.

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:11.440
Blames me for ruining his life.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:12.740
- Hm, why you?

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.340
- I was the lead prosecutor
on his rape trial.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.350
I was also the one who made
the original promise not-

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:18.390
- Why are you talking to the police

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:19.590
without a lawyer present?

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.300
I mean, sure, it would look a little weird

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.700
if a district attorney asked
for their own attorney,

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:29.640
but still, you don't
wanna talk to the police

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.600
if you have potentially some involvement

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.220
in a criminal homicide.

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.480
- At the time, there was
only one victim, Nicole,

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:37.420
and it was a tough case.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:38.640
There wasn't a lot of evidence.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.000
So, I offered him
immunity for that one case

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.590
so that Nicole's lawyer could depose him.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.370
- So, you're the reason he went to prison

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:45.770
and the reason he was released.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:46.810
- All right, this is really interesting.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.830
See, this actually did happen
in the Bill Cosby prosecution.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:54.270
The prosecuting attorney made a statement

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.930
that they weren't going
to prosecute Cosby,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.410
and then later on due process grounds,

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.170
he was able to get out of
prison after three years,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.410
claiming that he relied on the
statement to the prosecution.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:04.660
Interesting fact,

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.840
the prosecutor at the
time was Bruce Castor Jr.,

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.700
who would go on to represent
former President Trump

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.550
in his impeachment hearings.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.810
But basically, and a lot of
people disagree with this,

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.450
the statements of the
prosecutor were the reason

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.560
that Bill Cosby was let out of jail.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:24.380
And so, we're basically
copying that fact circumstance

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.940
in this prosecution as well.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:28.430
- Why did you and Henry
King meet the other day?

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:31.360
- He said he was going to
destroy me, destroy my family,

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.090
and I, I just lost it.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:36.180
- And because of your mistake,

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.993
he's walking out of jail a free man.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.000
- What are you really
asking me, detective,

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.650
if I killed Henry King?

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:44.483
- Did you?

00:05:47.041 --> 00:05:48.641
- Get the hell out of my office.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.660
- Ooh, she doesn't answer
the question, there,

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:55.630
and also did not have
legal representation.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:58.220
If you're talking to the police,
get legal representation.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.480
- Hey, got a hit.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:02.170
Here's King's wife on the
corner of 78th and Columbus,

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:03.410
two blocks from her house.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:04.933
Timestamp says 9:33.

00:06:06.084 --> 00:06:08.858
- That's 27 minutes
before Henry got popped.

00:06:08.858 --> 00:06:11.170
(cell clanging)

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:14.190
- I didn't lie, I just
left out a few details.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:15.310
- That's what lawyers would call

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:17.600
a distinction without a difference.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.140
- After dinner, I started walking home,

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:22.210
but I knew Henry would still be awake

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.190
and I couldn't bear it.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.590
So, I went to a bar a
block away, Maxwell's.

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.140
Had a few drinks.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.050
- Okay, so without a lawyer,

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.080
you are admitting that what you told

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.320
the police previously was a lie,

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.250
and now you are also saying

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.913
that you have animosity
towards the victim.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:44.633
Yeah, not the brightest on this one.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:47.867
- This is probably nothing,

00:06:47.867 --> 00:06:51.330
but I did notice someone hanging out

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.540
at the end of the block a few times.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:57.510
He had on a hoodie, green, I think.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.810
And he was wearing sunglasses
even though it was dark.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:04.160
- Okay, so she was drinking,

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:05.360
she left out a bunch of details,

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.140
and then she's able to
remember a mundane fact like,

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:11.830
there was a person with a hoodie

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:13.200
on the night of the murder.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.587
I mean, it's kinda like the
Simpsons episode where they ask,

00:07:15.587 --> 00:07:17.970
"You remember a boy
with a piece of paper?"

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.530
Like, "Yeah, you don't
forget a fact like that!"

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:21.363
- When Milhouse left,

00:07:21.363 --> 00:07:23.540
did you notice if he was
carrying a piece of paper?

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:26.847
- Oh, yeah, you don't
forget a thing like that.

00:07:26.847 --> 00:07:29.020
- Got a hit from the DNA.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:32.420
A woman named Nicole Bell,
maiden name Nicole Atkins.

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:33.950
- Wait, okay. (laughs)

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:35.870
Is this the next day, the next week,

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:37.250
is this months in,

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:41.710
surely you can't get a DNA
match just, like, immediately!

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:45.700
And even then, is this person
going to be in the database?

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.210
I mean, (laughs)

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.470
this seems like some real
"CSI" nonsense right here.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:54.700
- Nicole Atkins was the first
woman to accuse Henry King.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:57.030
- Oh, called it!

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:00.140
It's one of the victims
committed the murder.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:00.973
Nailed it.

00:08:00.973 --> 00:08:04.280
- Tuesday night I was
home with my husband.

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:05.920
- Why are you talking to the police

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:07.170
without a lawyer present?

00:08:08.388 --> 00:08:12.210
Ugh, that's, "Law &amp; Order"'s
gonna drive me insane!

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:14.530
- No one in this building
thinks you did anything wrong.

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:16.600
Hell, if it were up to me, I'd
give you the key to the city.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:17.870
The other thing you gotta understand

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:20.290
is we've got the whole thing on video.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:22.550
Holding the Sig 380,

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:26.130
waiting outside the service
entrance, shooting King.

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:30.790
- Okay, so, the police
are allowed to lie to you.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:33.710
This has gone up to the
Supreme Court many times

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:35.780
and the Supreme Court says that the police

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:37.810
are under no obligation
to tell you the truth.

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:40.170
It's popular belief that the police

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:41.220
have to tell you that they're police

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:42.053
and they can't lie to you.

00:08:42.053 --> 00:08:43.110
That's total nonsense.

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:45.400
The police can lie to
you all that they want.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:49.700
So, what he's doing here
passes constitutional muster.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:51.270
- We already know what happened.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.200
Why are we even talking?

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:54.220
'Cause they're lying to you

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.530
and you don't have a lawyer, dummy.

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:59.130
- Tell us your side of the
story so we can help you.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:05.220
- Then what?

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.070
- Nicole, you move on with your life.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:10.562
I give you my word.

00:09:10.562 --> 00:09:13.250
- (laughs) Again, this is probably getting

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:14.083
close to the line,

00:09:14.083 --> 00:09:15.840
but I don't think that that violates

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:17.630
any constitutional issues, here.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:19.700
- Well, you saw his TV interview, right?

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:21.100
There was no remorse, none.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.123
It was like he was mocking us.

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:25.120
I couldn't take it.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.000
- Yeah, then she confesses, okay.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:27.900
- Yes, I shot him.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:30.640
- Nicole Bell, You're
under arrest for murder.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:31.640
- What?

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:32.790
You just said-

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:33.877
- I lied.

00:09:33.877 --> 00:09:35.440
- And of course, right.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.210
She's surprised because
she's being arrested

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:38.400
after she confessed to the crime.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.030
Now, you might be wondering,

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:44.840
was she given her Miranda rights
before this interrogation?

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:46.770
And the answer is probably no

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:49.580
because the police are just
doing an investigation.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.420
Miranda rights kick in only
when you are dealing with

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:54.280
what's called a custodial interrogation,

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.360
meaning that you have
already been arrested.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:59.530
So, generally, when someone is arrested,

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:01.560
they are read their Miranda
rights at the same time.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.090
But if the police are interviewing you

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.130
and they haven't arrested
you, you're free to go,

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:07.760
then you're not in police custody

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.060
and the police can still use
your confession against you

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:13.860
even if you haven't gotten
Miranda rights at that point,

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.510
presumably, we don't know exactly

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:16.470
what happened in this investigation,

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:17.700
but that's the way that
they're portraying it.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.320
So, I don't think the
lack of Miranda rights

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:22.680
at this particular time is an issue

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.960
with respect to this confession.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.010
- Keller filed a motion

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:27.500
to suppress Nicole Bell's confession.

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:28.467
- On what basis?

00:10:28.467 --> 00:10:30.760
- Defendant was improperly Mirandized,

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:32.480
confession wasn't known and voluntary,

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:35.430
police used improper and coercive tactics.

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:36.493
- Did Cosgrove lie?

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:39.483
- Yeah.

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:42.260
Did a good job, too.

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:43.130
- Yeah, who cares?

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:45.820
Well, I mean morally, whatever,

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:48.820
but why does this district attorney care

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:51.800
whether the interrogating
police officer lied or not?

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:53.770
- This case is front page news, Nolan.

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:54.820
- I get it.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:58.033
But with all due respect,
that's not relevant.

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:02.527
When you asked me to come here, you said,

00:11:02.527 --> 00:11:05.207
"I need someone who sees the world

00:11:05.207 --> 00:11:06.740
"through a different lens."

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:08.950
- The district attorney is recruiting

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:12.100
specific assistant district attorneys

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:14.790
to see things through a different lens?

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:16.930
What is that nonsense?

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:17.870
No way!

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:20.060
- But it's a legal confession, Nolan.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.360
Cops are allowed to lie.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:25.200
- They are, but it makes the
confession less reliable.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:26.270
- Why?

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:27.103
- No, it, no!

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:31.730
What version of ethics are
they going to go with, here,

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:35.320
that a constitutional, legal
method of interrogation

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:38.640
that got them the confession was, ugh!

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.100
So what, it was used?

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.410
No way, I don't buy this for a second.

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:44.620
- Cosgrove spun the suspect upside-down.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.320
He practically promised her immunity,

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:49.350
told her that no one in the DA's office

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:51.540
would even consider prosecuting her.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:52.373
Why?

00:11:52.373 --> 00:11:55.370
Let the defense tear him apart on cross,

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.490
shift the focus away from the evidence,

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:59.410
and onto her sympathetic client

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:02.190
and the big, bad police department.

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:05.130
I just wanna do what
is best for this case.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:07.923
- No, that is, that's so wrong.

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:11.124
- Okay, then it's out.

00:12:11.124 --> 00:12:13.200
- (blows raspberry) How?

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:14.033
- Thank you.

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:18.270
- I mean, if he's not going
to use the confession,

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.420
then you assign another
assistant district attorney

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:22.000
to prosecute this case.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:23.163
Just yank him off.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:26.970
Okay, so this is February.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:29.770
Two months after the murder,
they're going to trial.

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:31.610
That's not a thing.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:32.730
That just doesn't happen.

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:33.563
No way.

00:12:33.563 --> 00:12:35.170
- Two blocks from the brownstone,

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:38.470
tossing the murder weapon into a dumpster.

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:42.840
As for motive, well, she's got a good one.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:45.760
When the justice system failed her,

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:49.410
the defendant took matters
into her own hands,

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:51.040
got justice her way.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:54.230
- I mean, that's a
classic opening statement.

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:55.350
You can't make an argument,

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:58.330
but you need to explain that just because

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:02.580
the victim of the murder
did some bad things,

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.200
that that doesn't entitle the defendant

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:07.410
to take law into her own hands.

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:10.180
And you know, you need to set that out

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:11.720
right in front of the jury.

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:13.640
So, he's doing an okay job.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:15.070
I think you could do a better job of it,

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:18.240
but you 100% would want to
get that out in the open

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.070
and just own it from the very beginning.

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.170
- Where did you recover the murder weapon?

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:24.692
- There.

00:13:24.692 --> 00:13:26.600
ECT found it in that dumpster.

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:30.350
- Detective, you have no idea
that the hoodie you recovered

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:31.183
is the actual hoodie

00:13:31.183 --> 00:13:33.157
that the person in the photo is wearing.

00:13:33.157 --> 00:13:34.620
- "You have no idea,"

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:37.400
no, that's a really bad question.

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:40.340
You would never, ever
phrase a question that way,

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:43.990
because that's not what
the witness is saying.

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:44.823
- Correct.

00:13:44.823 --> 00:13:45.955
- Whoa, what?

00:13:45.955 --> 00:13:48.890
You're, what are you talking about?

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:49.900
Yes, you have an idea,

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:51.100
because you can use your eyes

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:52.290
and you can see the
one that's in the photo

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.130
and you can compare it to
the one that's in court,

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:55.580
and they look really similar.

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:59.010
So, that's a really dumb question to ask

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:01.040
and that's a really dumb
way to answer that question.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:03.290
- Okay, so help me out, here.

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:06.440
If someone is wearing a
sweatshirt and shoots a man

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:07.960
five times at close range,

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:11.310
there would likely be blood
spatter all over it, correct?

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:12.350
- Not necessarily.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:14.280
And like I said, when we found the hoodie,

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:16.310
it was damp like she just washed it.

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:17.450
- [Keller] But.

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:18.290
- Ah, man.

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:21.670
So, the classic blunder
in cross-examination

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:23.220
is you're trying to get the witness

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:25.000
to agree to your conclusions.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:27.200
You just lay the foundation.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:29.730
This hoodie did not have any blood on it.

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.970
And then, with an expert witness later on,

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:33.130
you would have them testify

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:35.300
that if someone was shot at close range,

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:36.880
that the blood would get on a hoodie,

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:39.000
and then you connect those dots later on.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.900
You don't try and do it
through an adverse witness!

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:43.100
This is bad.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:44.720
- You have absolutely no evidence

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.280
that Nicole Bell committed this crime,

00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:49.120
just some random blood-free hoodie

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:50.970
that vaguely resembles the hoodie-

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:53.040
- No, that's not what he's testifying to.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:54.200
- No, that's not what I'm saying.

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:57.270
The woman in that green
hoodie by that dumpster

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:58.740
is Nicole Bell.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:00.780
- Detective, please refrain
from drawing conclusions.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:01.860
- I apologize, your honor.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:03.500
I'm not drawing conclusions,

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:06.080
because the defendant actually told me

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:08.670
she tossed the murder
weapon into that dumpster

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:10.033
when she confessed that she shot-

00:15:10.033 --> 00:15:11.420
- Objection!

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:12.950
- Sustained.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:16.100
Counsel, my chambers, now.

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:18.060
So, what the hell is going on here, Price?

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:18.940
- As I said, your honor,

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:21.070
Detective Cosgrove made an honest mistake.

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:22.120
- I know Cosgrove.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:23.700
He's smart, he doesn't
make honest mistakes.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.660
Whatever he said, he said for a reason.

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:26.930
- It's possible.

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:28.270
It's still my fault.

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:29.450
I should have reminded Cosgrove

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:30.740
that the confession was inadmissible.

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:31.800
I apologize.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:34.100
But his testimony wasn't
overly prejudicial.

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:36.650
A curative instruction can fix this.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:41.000
- Ah, that seems unlikely.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:42.260
I mean, what he's asking for is the judge

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:45.180
to instruct the jury
to forget and disregard

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:48.398
that they heard this
talk about confession.

00:15:48.398 --> 00:15:50.200
But that seems awfully prejudicial.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:51.870
But the thing is,

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:56.500
this confession was
not illegally obtained,

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:58.620
so even if you decided as the prosecutor

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:01.730
not to introduce this evidence,

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:02.820
I don't think you would ever make

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:05.770
an agreement with the defense
that would tie your hands,

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:08.030
in case something like this happened.

00:16:08.030 --> 00:16:09.600
- I agree.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:11.920
I'll advise the jury to
disregard Cosgrove's statement.

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:12.850
- What?

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:14.330
No mistrial?

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:16.393
The jury cannot unhear
what Cosgrove just said!

00:16:16.393 --> 00:16:19.483
- They'll have to, because
we are moving forward.

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.960
- The problem is it's grounds for appeal.

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:26.080
So, I would not wanna continue
prosecuting this case,

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:27.180
knowing that the court of appeals

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.290
might just turn it around like that

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:31.620
because of this prejudicial
information that came out.

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:35.250
- He poured me a glass of
wine and I just stared at it.

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:37.710
I was afraid he might
have put something in it.

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:40.640
He saw that I was nervous
and that infuriated him.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:44.010
He, he started berating
me, so I ran outside.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.460
He followed me and grabbed me.

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:47.910
I thought he was going to
kill me, so I shot him!

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:52.650
Henry King ruined my life.

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:55.473
He ruined 39 other lives, too.

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.820
And some judge just let him walk free!

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:00.130
- Objection, she's making a speech.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:02.570
- So, I shot him, so-

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:06.400
- I mean, making a speech,
that's not an objection.

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.420
I guess he's saying she's
lapsing into a narrative,

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:11.800
but you don't make that objection

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:14.920
when this kind of a witness is testifying.

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:17.440
- Did the defendant tell you

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:20.142
she'd planned to kill Henry King?

00:17:20.142 --> 00:17:22.450
(ominous music)

00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:23.880
- I refuse to answer on the grounds

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:25.243
that it may incriminate me.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:31.210
- Okay, so this would
cause all kinds of problems

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:33.100
with the trial, here.

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:35.310
I mean, obviously you're not allowed to

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:38.610
use someone's statement

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:41.120
that they refuse to
testify against themselves.

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:44.220
I actually don't know if
that applies to third parties

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:47.160
or if the jury can take
an adverse inference,

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:50.020
given that some witness
is refusing to testify

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:52.090
on the grounds that it
might incriminate them.

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:54.650
Yeah, this is a disaster for the defense.

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:55.910
This is just really, really bad.

00:17:55.910 --> 00:17:57.600
I don't think this is bad
for the prosecution at all.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:02.600
- The jury wants to acquit
irrespective of the facts,

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:04.363
irrespective of the law.

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:08.992
Too bad we didn't have a damn confession.

00:18:08.992 --> 00:18:13.390
- (laughs) Yeah, that would've been good.

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:18.220
- You told me a story a while back

00:18:20.322 --> 00:18:21.263
about your family.

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:26.220
- You're saying you want
me to deliver the closing?

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:29.770
I'm a prosecutor.

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:33.170
I am sworn to enforce
the laws of the state.

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:38.170
Yet I understand the
defendant's desire for revenge.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:43.420
Nine years ago, my sister
was raped and murdered.

00:18:43.489 --> 00:18:45.690
- Ah, I, d'oh.

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:47.580
I don't think you're allowed to bring in

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.595
that kind of a personal
anecdote on closing argument.

00:18:50.595 --> 00:18:53.590
That seems incorrect, right there.

00:18:53.590 --> 00:18:56.110
- When Henry King was
released from prison,

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:59.163
the defendant's thirst
for revenge escalated.

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:03.730
Maybe she even began to
fantasize about killing him.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:07.840
- That's, ah, that seems like
an awful lot of speculation,

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:09.800
even for closing argument.

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:13.313
- This case comes down
to one simple question.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:19.640
Did Nicole Bell intentionally
shoot and kill Henry King?

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:27.707
If the answer is yes, you must convict.

00:19:27.707 --> 00:19:30.290
(somber music)

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:34.610
- On the count of murder
in the second degree.

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:37.540
- Wait, why would it be
murder in the second degree?

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:41.280
I mean, this was premeditated.

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:42.310
I mean, she planned it out,

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:46.070
and that was the entire
prosecution's argument, here.

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:49.220
It really should be murder
in the first degree.

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:50.530
I don't think there's any,

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:52.150
like, yes, it's a lesser offense.

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:56.820
But if you prove up all of
the elements of murder one

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:57.910
and then you go for murder two,

00:19:57.910 --> 00:19:59.613
it's gonna confuse the jury that,

00:20:00.466 --> 00:20:02.960
ugh, it should be murder
in the first degree.

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:04.083
- Guilty.

00:20:04.083 --> 00:20:07.000
(people murmuring)

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:15.923
- [Nolan] Jury got it, right, Sam.

00:20:16.830 --> 00:20:17.867
- Doesn't feel right.

00:20:17.867 --> 00:20:19.360
- And now she's showing remorse,

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:21.810
but it was absolutely the right decision.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:23.090
They would've used the confession

00:20:23.090 --> 00:20:25.740
and they would have no
problem about this conviction.

00:20:26.745 --> 00:20:28.180
This is weird.

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:29.223
This is silly.

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:31.710
- The only thing that will let me sleep

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:35.550
is you requesting the
lightest sentence possible.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:36.673
- What, no!

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:38.690
Never.

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:41.600
Okay, well, at least
"Law &amp; Order" is back!

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:44.170
Now it's time to give it
a grade for legal realism.

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:46.950
On the one hand, you have basically a case

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:49.300
that parallels the Bill Cosby case.

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:50.860
Issues involving immunity,

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:52.820
lying to the victim,

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:55.840
and the constitutional issues
that come as a result of that.

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:57.820
On the other hand,

00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:00.660
basically everyone's
motivation makes no sense,

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:02.520
the strategy makes no sense,

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:06.110
and none of this would
ever happen because the DAs

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:09.060
would not have moral
issues with any of this.

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:11.950
So, on the whole, God, it pains me,

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:15.577
but I have to say this
is probably C or C-.

00:21:16.427 --> 00:21:17.780
I'm thrilled that law order is back,

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:21.020
but this was terribly unrealistic.

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:22.820
And apropos of this episode,

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:24.700
there's been a lot of
discussion about the potential

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:26.350
to end Roe v. Wade,

00:21:26.350 --> 00:21:28.600
and it often feels like
there's nothing that we can do.

00:21:28.600 --> 00:21:29.770
But that's not true.

